1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,360
Tomás Ferreira: Thanks everyone. This is the scope call for the discharge summary
drafting assistant at Calder Valley.

2
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:17,520
Amara Okafor: Let me frame the problem for the group. Nurses spend roughly forty
minutes per patient on discharge paperwork, and that is time away from
the bedside.

3
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:26,020
Joaquin Reyes: And it is the same facts typed three times, in the chart, in the summary
and in the patient instructions.

4
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:32,780
Tomás Ferreira: So the assistant drafts and the nurse reviews. Let's start with privacy
since it shapes everything.

5
00:00:33,980 --> 00:00:39,060
Beth Calloway: The rule is simple. Nothing containing patient identifiers can leave the
hospital network.

6
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:44,280
Beth Calloway: That includes logs, crash reports and any telemetry your tooling sends
home.

7
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:49,080
Tomás Ferreira: Understood, we will keep that in mind for the design.

8
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:57,360
Amara Okafor: On review, a clinician must review and sign every draft before it enters
the chart. No auto-filing, ever.

9
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:03,560
Joaquin Reyes: The nurse signs for the nursing content and the physician signs for the
medical content.

10
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:08,460
Tomás Ferreira: So two signatures on some drafts. We can model that.

11
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:10,900
Tomás Ferreira: Where do we start?

12
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:18,260
Amara Okafor: We will begin on the cardiac step-down unit only. It has the most
predictable discharge patterns.

13
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:22,400
Joaquin Reyes: Thirty beds, about four discharges a day on average.

14
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,840
Tomás Ferreira: And what are we assuming for the EHR integration?

15
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:35,840
Amara Okafor: We are assuming the EHR integration team can give you read access to
discharge orders by the end of October.

16
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:39,160
Beth Calloway: That is optimistic. Their queue is long.

17
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:42,260
Amara Okafor: I will push on it.

18
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:50,100
Joaquin Reyes: One more thing on format. Drafts must follow the discharge template
version 4, because the auditors check against it.

19
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:53,140
Tomás Ferreira: Template version 4, noted.

20
00:01:53,640 --> 00:02:00,520
Amara Okafor: Someone asked if we could also draft transfer summaries. Maybe later,
but nobody has signed up for that.

21
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:04,240
Tomás Ferreira: Understood, we will not discuss it today.

22
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:10,720
Beth Calloway: Can I ask what happens when the assistant is not sure about something?

23
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:16,200
Tomás Ferreira: Good question. We can look at how uncertain fields are shown during
design.

24
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:21,100
Joaquin Reyes: I would like to see that before we go further.

25
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,300
Beth Calloway: Same here.

26
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:29,940
Amara Okafor: Then I think we have the scope. Tomás, please send us the written list.

27
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:33,000
Tomás Ferreira: Will do by Monday. Thanks everyone.

28
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,400
Beth Calloway: Thanks.

29
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,400
Joaquin Reyes: Thank you.

30
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,400
Amara Okafor: Thank you all.
